===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰreg-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|breken}}, from {{inh|en|ang|brecan||to break}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*brekan}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*brekaną||to break}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*bʰreg-||to break}}. The word is a {{doublet|en|bray|nocap=1}}.
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognates of Germanic origin include {{cog|sco|brek||to break}}, {{cog|fy|brekke||to break}}, {{cog|nl|breken||to break}}, {{cog|nds|breken||to break}}, {{cog|de|brechen||to break}}, {{cog|fr|broyer||to crush, grind}}, {{cog|got|𐌱𐍂𐌹𐌺𐌰𐌽|t=to break, destroy}}, {{cog|no|brek||desire, yearning}}.

Also cognate with {{cog|sq|brishtë||fragile}}, {{cog|la|frangō||break, break up, shatter|pos=verb}}, whence {{cog|en|fracture}} and other terms – {{m|en|fragile}}, {{m|en|frail}}, {{m|en|fraction}}, and {{m|en|fragment}}.
{{col-bottom}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|breaks|breaking|broke|past2=brake|past2_qual=archaic|broken|past_ptc2=broke|past_ptc2_qual=nonstandard}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To [[separate]] into two or more [[piece]]s, to [[fracture]] or [[crack]], by a process that cannot easily be [[reverse]]d for [[reassembly]].
#: {{ux|en|If the vase falls to the floor, it might '''break'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|In order to tend to the accident victim, he will '''break''' the window of the car.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=8 May 2012|page=79|title=Ottolenghi: The Cookbook|author=w:Yotam Ottolenghi; Sami Tamimi|publisher=w:Random House|url= https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=WFzJR9ZZ7aEC|isbn=9781446407097|passage=First, marinate the tofu. In a bowl, whisk the kecap manis, chilli sauce, and sesame oil together. Cut the tofu into strips about 1cm thick, mix gently (so it doesn't '''break''') with the marinade and leave in the fridge for half an hour.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To [[crack]] or [[fracture]] (bone) under a [[physical]] [[strain]].
##: {{ux|en|His ribs '''broke''' under the weight of the rocks piled on his chest.}}
##: {{ux|en|She '''broke''' her neck.}}
##: {{ux|en|He slipped on the ice and '''broke''' his leg.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[divide]] (something, often [[money]]) into smaller units.
#: {{ux|en|Can you '''break''' a hundred-dollar bill for me?}}
#: {{ux|en|The wholesaler '''broke''' the container loads into palettes and boxes for local retailers.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (a person or animal) to lose [[spirit]] or [[will]]; to [[crush]] the spirits of.
#: {{ux|en|Her child’s death '''broke''' Angela.}}
#: {{ux|en|Interrogators have used many forms of torture to '''break''' prisoners of war.}}
#: {{ux|en|The interrogator hoped to '''break''' her to get her testimony against her accomplices.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Job|9|16|17|If I had called, and had answered me, yet would I not beleeue that he had hearkened vnto my voice: For he '''breaketh''' me with a tempest, and multiplieth my wounds without cause.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 8|IV|2|text=An old man, '''broken''' with the storms of state,<br>Is come to lay his weary bones among ye;<br>Give him a little earth for charity}}
## To turn an [[animal]] into a [[beast of burden]].
##* {{quote-text|en|year=2002|author=w:John Fusco|title={{w|Spirit: Stallion of the Cimarron}}
|passage=Colonel: See, gentlemen? Any horse could be '''broken'''.}}
##: {{ux|en|You have to '''break''' an elephant before you can use it as an animal of burden.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[crush]]ed, or [[overwhelmed]] with [[sorrow]] or [[grief]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1 Feb 86|volume=13|number=28|journal=Gay Community News|page=5|author=Kathy Jo Elliott|title=Solid As A Rock|text=Two days later they transferred her to another prison to separate us. I '''broke'''. My life was gone, so I thought.}}
#: {{ux|en|My heart is '''breaking'''.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[interrupt]]; to [[destroy]] the [[continuity]] of; to [[dissolve]] or [[terminate]].
#: {{ux|en|I’ve got to '''break''' this habit I have of biting my nails.}}
#: {{co|en|to '''break''' silence; to '''break''' one's sleep; to '''break''' one’s journey}}
#: {{ux|en|I had won four games in a row, but now you've '''broken''' my streak of luck.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Tempest|V|i|passage=Go, release them, Ariel; / My charms I'll '''break''', their senses I'll restore.}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|theatre}} To end the run of (a play).
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1958|author=Walter Macqueen-Pope|title=St. James's: Theatre of Distinction|page=134
|passage=In July Alexander '''broke''' the run and went on tour, as was his custom. He believed in keeping in touch with provincial audiences and how wise he was!}}
##* {{quote-text|en|year=1986|author=Kurt Gänzl|title=The British Musical Theatre: 1865-1914|page=610
|passage=After Camberwell he '''broke''' the play's season and brought it back in the autumn with a few revisions and a noticeably strengthened cast but without any special success.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[ruin]] [[financially]].
#: {{ux|en|The recession '''broke''' some small businesses.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Juvenal Satires|7|passage=With arts like these rich Matho, when he speaks, / Attracts all fees, and little lawyers '''breaks'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To [[fail]] in [[business]]; to go [[broke]], to become [[bankrupt]].
#* {{RQ:Bacon Essayes|chapter=Of Riches|passage=He that puts all upon adventures doth oftentimes '''break''', and come to poverty.}}
#* '''1791-92''', {{w|Jane Austen}}, ‘A Collection of Letters’, ''Juvenilia'':
#*: ‘I knew he was in some such low way—He '''broke''' did not he?’
# {{lb|en|finance|intransitive}} Of [[price]]s on the [[stock exchange]]: to [[fall]] suddenly.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=George Angell|title=Small Stocks for Big Profits
|passage=With a few exceptions, stock prices tend to follow the overall market averages. When you have a market decline, therefore, many stocks share the same overall chart pattern. Prices '''break''' and go sideways for a period of time.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[violate]]; to fail to [[adhere]] to.
#: {{ux|en|When you go to Vancouver, promise me you won't '''break''' the law.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''broke''' his vows by cheating on his wife.}}
#: {{co|en|to '''break''' one’s word}}
#: {{ux|en|Time travel would '''break''' the laws of physics.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|book=Samson Agonistes|48|lines=749–751|passage=Out, out ''Hyæna''; theſe are thy wonted arts, [...] To '''break''' all faith, all vows, deceive, betray,}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a fever}} To go [[down]], in terms of [[temperature]], [[indicate|indicating]] that the most [[dangerous]] part of the [[illness]] has [[pass]]ed.
#: {{ux|en|Susan's fever '''broke''' at about 3 AM, and the doctor said the worst was over.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a|_|spell of settled|_|weather}} To [[end]].
#: {{ux|en|The forecast says the hot weather will '''break''' by midweek.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a|_|storm}} To [[begin]] or [[end]].
#: {{ux|en|We ran to find shelter before the storm '''broke'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Around midday the storm '''broke''', and the afternoon was calm and sunny.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of morning, dawn, day etc.}} To [[arrive]].
#: {{ux|en|Morning has '''broken'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The day '''broke''' crisp and clear.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|II|ii|passage=The day begins to '''break''', and night is fled.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|gaming|_|slang}} To [[render]] (a game) [[unchallenging]] by [[altering]] its [[rule]]s or [[exploit]]ing [[loophole]]s or [[weakness]]es in them in a way that gives a [[player]] an [[unfair]] [[advantage]].
#: {{ux|en|Changing the rules to let white have three extra queens would '''break''' chess.}}
#: {{ux|en|I '''broke''' the RPG by training every member of my party to cast fireballs as well as use swords.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|intransitive}} To [[stop]], or to cause to stop, [[function]]ing [[properly]] or [[altogether]].
#: {{ux|en|On the hottest day of the year the refrigerator '''broke'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|Did you two '''break''' the trolley by racing with it?}}
## {{lb|en|specifically|in|_|programming}} To cause (some [[feature]] of a [[program]] or piece of [[software]]) to [[stop]] [[function]]ing [[properly]]; to cause a [[regression]].
##: {{ux|en|Adding 64-bit support '''broke''' backward compatibility with earlier versions.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (a [[barrier]]) to no longer [[bar]].
#: {{co|en|to '''break''' a seal}}
## {{lb|en|specifically}} To cause the [[shell]] of (an [[egg]]) to [[crack]], so that the [[inside]] ([[yolk]]) is [[accessible]].
## {{lb|en|specifically}} To [[open]] (a [[safe]]) without using the [[correct]] [[key]], [[combination]]{{,}} or the like.
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1992|artist={{w|Alice in Chains|Alice in Chains}}|title={{w|Dirt (Alice in Chains album)|Rain When I Die}}|passage=I'm a riddle so strong, you can't '''break''' me}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[destroy]] the [[arrangement]] of; to [[throw]] into [[disorder]]; to [[pierce]].
#: {{ux|en|The cavalry were not able to '''break''' the British squares.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a wave of water}} To collapse into [[surf]], after arriving in [[shallow]] water. [[Image:A storm at Pors-Loubous.jpg|right|thumb|196px|A wave '''breaking'''.]]
#* {{quote-av|1=en|year=1966|director={{w|Bruce Brown (director)|Bruce Brown}}|title={{w|The Endless Summer}}|text=There are many places on the reef that '''break''' during the summer.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[burst forth]]; to make its way; to come into [[view]].
#* {{RQ:Dryden Metamorphoses|book=I|section=The Gyants War|page=16|passage=The Clouds are ſtill above; and, while I ſpeak, / A ſecond deluge o'er our head may '''break'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1800|author=w:William Wordsworth|title=[[s:Lyrical Ballads (1800)/Volume 2/The Fountain|The Fountain]]
|passage=And from the turf a fountain '''broke''', / And gurgled at our feet.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[interrupt]] or [[cease]] one's [[work]] or [[occupation]] [[temporarily]]; to go on [[#Noun|break]].
#: {{ux|en|Let's '''break''' for lunch.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[interrupt]] (a [[fall]]) by [[insert]]ing something so that the falling object does not (immediately) [[hit]] something else [[beneath]].
#: {{ux|en|He survived the jump out the window because the bushes below '''broke''' his fall.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|ergative}} To [[disclose]] or [[make known]] an item of [[news]], a [[band]], etc.
#: {{ux|en|The newsman wanted to '''break''' a big story, something that would make him famous.}}
#: {{ux|en|I don’t know how to '''break''' this to you, but your cat is not coming back.}}
#: {{ux|en|When news of their divorce '''broke'''...}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2010|author=Jon Kutner; Spencer Leigh|title=1,000 UK Number One Hits
|passage=Herman's Hermits version of 'I'm Into Something Good' topped the UK charts and also '''broke''' the band in the States.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023-03-08|author=Jonathan Tannenwald|title=CBS is launching a 24/7 soccer channel online|journal=w:The Philadelphia Inquirer|url=https://www.inquirer.com/soccer/cbs-sports-soccer-live-streaming-golazo-network-20230308.html|passage=“If something '''breaks''' during the day, work hours up until the evening, we’ll cut in — if we’re in the middle of, let’s say, a magazine program or a podcast on tape or a re-air of the game,” Radovich said.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a [[sound]]}} To become [[audible]] [[suddenly]].
#* {{circa|1843}}, {{w|George Lippard}}, ''The Battle-Day of Germantown'', reprinted in ''Washington and His Generals "1776"'', page 45&nbsp;[http://google.com/books?id=EM-qNjWrI9YC&pg=PA45&dq=%22sound+of+musquetry%22]:
#*: Like the crash of thunderbolts[...], the sound of musquetry '''broke''' over the lawn, [...].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[change]] a [[steady]] [[state]] [[abrupt]]ly.
#: {{ux|en|His coughing '''broke''' the silence.}}
#: {{ux|en|His turning on the lights '''broke''' the enchantment.}}
#: {{ux|en|With the mood '''broken''', what we had been doing seemed pretty silly.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|with '''for'''}} To (attempt to) [[disengage]] and [[flee]] to; to [[make a run for it|make a run for]].
#* {{quote-av|en|author=Drachinifel|title=Last Ride of the High Seas Fleet - Battle of Texel 1918|date=17 October 2018|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YrTeYcRXDEw|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220804035059/https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YrTeYcRXDEw|archivedate=4 August 2022|accessdate=4 August 2022|time=26:02|text=As the last firing of the big guns begins to die down, the German light forces still fighting to the west begin to make their choices.  Some '''break''' for the open sea; others run for the German-occupied coast; still others stand and die.  A small group decide to strike their colors, in imitation of three of the larger German ships.}}
# {{lb|en|copulative|informal}} To [[suddenly]] [[become]].
#: {{ux|en|Things began '''breaking''' bad for him when his parents died.}}
#: {{ux|en|The arrest was standard, when suddenly the suspect '''broke''' ugly.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a [[male]] [[voice]]}} To become [[deep]]er at [[puberty]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a [[voice]]}} To [[alter]] in type due to [[emotion]] or [[strain]]: in [[men]], generally to go [[up]], in [[women]], sometimes to go [[down]]; to [[crack]].
#: {{ux|en|His voice '''breaks''' when he gets emotional.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a [[sauce]] or [[emulsion]]}} To [[de-emulsify]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|title=Encyclopedia of supramolecular chemistry|volume=2|page=1466|author=J. L. Atwood; Jonathan W. Steed|passage=Conversely, as the emulsion '''breaks''' and the system returns to the original state, energy is released.|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=pcQSir2QXbMC}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Emulsions and emulsion stability|volume=22|author=Johan Sjöblom|page=400|year=2006|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=vcNWGexVc4cC|passage=When the droplets hit a solid wall the emulsion '''breaks''' instantly forming a bitumen on the wall and thus a layer up to 1 cm thick can be sprayed in one operation without requiring drying in between.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[surpass]] or do [[better]] than (a [[specific]] [[number]]); to do [[better]] than (a [[record]]), setting a new record.
#: {{ux|en|He '''broke''' the men's 100-meter record.}}
#: {{ux|en|I can't believe she '''broke''' 3 under par!}}
#: {{ux|en|The policeman '''broke''' sixty on a residential street in his hurry to catch the thief.}}
# {{lb|en|sports|and|games}}:
## {{lb|en|transitive|tennis}} To [[win]] a [[game]] (against one's [[opponent]]) as [[receiver]].
##: {{ux|en|He needs to '''break''' serve to win the match.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=June 28, 2012|author=Jamie Jackson|title=Wimbledon 2012: Lukas Rosol shocked by miracle win over Rafael Nadal|work=the Guardian|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/sport/2012/jun/28/rafael-nadal-lukas-rosol-wimbledon-2012?INTCMP=ILCNETTXT3487|passage=Yet when play restarted the Czech was a train that kept on running over Nadal. After '''breaking''' Nadal in the opening game of the final set, he went 2-0 up and later took the count to 4-2 with yet another emphatic ace – one of his 22 throughout.}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive|billiards|snooker|pool}} To make the [[first]] [[shot]]; to [[scatter]] the [[ball]]s from the [[initial]] [[neat]] [[arrangement]].
##: {{ux|en|Is it your or my turn to '''break'''?}}
## {{lb|en|transitive|backgammon}} To [[remove]] one of the two [[man|men]] on (a [[point]]).
# {{lb|en|transitive|military|most often in the|_|passive tense}} To [[demote]]; to [[reduce]] the [[military]] [[rank]] of.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1926|author=w:T.E. Lawrence|title=Seven Pillars of Wisdom|location=New York|publisher=Anchor|year_published=1991|page=167
|passage=Sir Reginald Wingate, High Commissioner in Egypt, was happy for the success of the work he had advocated for years. I grudged him this happiness; for McMahon, who took the actual risk of starting it, had been '''broken''' just before prosperity began.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=February 9 1953|title=Books: First Rulers of Asia|titleurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20101030210729/http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,889691,00.html|journal=Time
|passage=And he played no favorites: when his son-in-law sacked a city he had been told to spare, Genghis '''broke''' him to private.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1968|author=w:William Manchester|title=w:The Arms of Krupp|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=TIsvPMXQEBcC&pg=PA215&dq=broken|page=215|publisher=Back Bay|year_published=2003|isbn=978-0-316-52940-2
|passage=One morning after the budget had failed to balance Finanzminister von Scholz picked up Der Reichsanzeiger and found he had been '''broken''' to sergeant.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=[[w:Peter Collier (political author)|Peter Collier]]|title=Medal of Honor: Portraits of Valor Beyond the Call of Duty|publisher=Second Edition, Artisan Books|isbn=978-1-57965-314-9|pageurl=http://books.google.com/books?id=EqH-BJ-k0NsC&pg=PA42&dq=broken|page=42
|passage=Not long after this event, Clausen became involved in another disciplinary situation and was '''broken''' to private—the only one to win the Medal of Honor in Vietnam.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[end]] (a [[connection]]); to [[disconnect]].
#: {{ux|en|The referee ordered the boxers to '''break''' the clinch.}}
#: {{ux|en|The referee '''broke''' the boxers' clinch.}}
#: {{ux|en|I couldn’t hear a thing he was saying, so I '''broke''' the connection and called him back.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|sports}} To [[counter-attack]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=December 28, 2010|author=Kevin Darlin|title=West Brom 1 - 3 Blackburn|work=BBC|url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/eng_prem/9308862.stm|passage=The Baggies almost hit back instantly when {{w|Graham Dorrans}} '''broke''' from midfield and pulled the trigger from 15 yards but Paul Robinson did superbly to tip the Scot's drive around the post.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[lay ope]]n, as a purpose; to [[disclose]], [[divulge]], or [[communicate]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|V|ii|passage=Katharine, '''break''' thy mind to me.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become [[weakened]] in [[constitution]] or [[faculties]]; to [[lose]] [[health]] or [[strength]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1731|publisher=w:Jonathan Swift|title=s:Verses on His Own Death
|passage=See how the dean begins to '''break'''; / Poor gentleman he droops apace.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[destroy]] the [[strength]], [[firmness]], or [[consistency]] of.
#: {{co|en|to '''break''' flax}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[destroy]] the [[official]] [[character]] and [[standing]] of; to [[cashier]]; to [[dismiss]].
#* '''January 11, 1711''', {{w|Jonathan Swift}}, ''The Examiner'' No. 24
#*: when I see a great officer '''broke'''.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make an [[abrupt]] or [[sudden]] [[change]]; to change [[gait]].
#: {{co|en|to '''break''' into a run or gallop}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|archaic}} To [[fall out]]; to [[terminate]] [[friendship]].
#* '''c. 1700''' {{w|Jeremy Collier}}, ''On Friendship''
#*: To '''break''' upon the score of danger or expense is to be mean and narrow-spirited.
# {{label|en|computing}} To [[terminate]] the [[execution]] of a [[program]] before [[normal]] [[completion]].
# {{label|en|programming}} To [[suspend]] the [[execution]] of a [[program]] during [[debugging]] so that the [[state]] of the [[program]] can be [[investigate]]d.
# {{lb|en|computing}} To cause, or allow the occurrence of, a [[line break]].
